  toasts now and add reminders later, you rewrite the plumbing. The substance is the time-based reminders; action
  feedback rides along nearly free. I'd skip anything speculative about future collaborators — no user model, no
  read/unread sync, no delivery preferences. Those are wrong to guess at and easy to add once a backend actually exists.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should actually trigger a notification? → Both, shared surface (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next fork, and it's the one that shapes every file we write.

  [1mThe fork:[0m right now [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is a single static file you can double-click to open — no build step, no dependencies,
  no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m. A notification centre with reactive state is exactly the kind of feature that tempts a framework.
  Whether we add one is a decision that's cheap to make now and annoying to reverse in either direction.

  - [1mVanilla JS, ES modules, no build[0m — split into [38;5;153mtask-store.js[39m, [38;5;153mnotifications.js[39m, [38;5;153mscheduler.js[39m, [38;5;153mui/[39m. Stays
    double-clickable, zero dependencies, matches what's here. Cost: you hand-write the DOM updates. For an app this size
    that's maybe 150 lines of rendering, not a burden. Testing needs a lightweight runner.
  - [1mVite + a small framework[0m — better ergonomics if this app grows a lot of interactive surface, familiar testing story
    via Vitest. Cost: a build step, a dependency tree, and the repo stops being a static page you can just open. Hard to
    argue for at the current size.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m vanilla ES modules. The whole app is a task list and a notification tray; a framework would be more
  infrastructure than product, and the module boundaries do the real work of keeping it comprehensible. If the app
  later grows enough interactive surface to justify a framework, the store/scheduler modules port over unchanged — it's
  the rendering layer you'd rewrite, and that's the cheap part.
